= Senescence =

'''Senescence''' refers broadly to biological deterioration associated with aging, but the word is used in at least two distinct ways.

'''Organismal senescence''' is the age-associated decline in physiological function, resilience, fertility, or survival probability across an organism's life.

'''Cellular senescence''' is a durable cell-state program in which a previously proliferation-capable cell exits the cell cycle and develops characteristic molecular and secretory changes.

These phenomena interact, but they are not synonyms. An old organism contains many cell types that are not senescent, and cellular senescence can occur in young organisms during development, wound healing, or stress responses.

== Organismal aging ==

Organismal aging is a progressive increase in vulnerability to dysfunction and death with age.

Common age-associated changes include:

* reduced tissue repair;
* altered immune function;
* loss of physiological reserve;
* increased risk of chronic disease;
* reduced fertility in many species;
* changing body composition.

Aging rates differ among species, populations, tissues, and individuals.

There is no single molecular clock that explains all senescence.

== Evolutionary theories ==

Evolutionary biology asks why aging persists despite natural selection.

Three influential frameworks are:

'''Mutation accumulation''' — late-acting harmful mutations can persist because selection against effects expressed late in life is weaker.

'''Antagonistic pleiotropy''' — alleles that improve early-life reproductive success can be favored even if they cause later-life harm.

'''Disposable soma''' — organisms face tradeoffs in energy allocation among reproduction, growth, and long-term maintenance.

These theories are compatible rather than mutually exclusive in all contexts.

They explain why aging can evolve, not every molecular mechanism of aging.

== Hallmarks of aging ==

The “hallmarks of aging” framework organizes recurring molecular and cellular processes associated with aging.

A 2023 update expanded the framework to twelve hallmarks:<ref name="hallmarks">[https://www.cell.com/cell/fulltext/S0092-8674(22)01377-0 López-Otín, C. et al. (2023), “Hallmarks of Aging: An Expanding Universe,” ''Cell'' 186(2), 243–278.]</ref>

* genomic instability;
* telomere attrition;
* epigenetic alterations;
* loss of proteostasis;
* disabled macroautophagy;
* deregulated nutrient sensing;
* mitochondrial dysfunction;
* cellular senescence;
* stem-cell exhaustion;
* altered intercellular communication;
* chronic inflammation;
* dysbiosis.

The hallmarks are a conceptual framework.

They are not twelve independent causes with equal importance in every organism or disease.

== Genomic instability ==

DNA is continually damaged by internal and external processes.

Repair systems correct much of this damage.

With age, mutations, chromosome alterations, and DNA damage responses can accumulate.

Genomic instability can contribute to:

* cancer;
* cell dysfunction;
* senescence;
* altered gene expression.

The causal relationships vary by tissue and damage type.

== Telomere attrition ==

Telomeres protect chromosome ends.

In many somatic human cells, telomeres shorten with repeated cell division.

Critically short or dysfunctional telomeres can activate DNA-damage checkpoints and cellular senescence.

Telomere length is not a universal aging clock.

Some long-lived cell types divide rarely, and telomere biology varies among species.

== Epigenetic alterations ==

Aging is associated with changes in:

* DNA methylation;
* histone modification;
* chromatin organization;
* transcription.

DNA-methylation patterns can be used to build '''epigenetic clocks''' that predict chronological age or age-related outcomes.

A predictive clock is not automatically a causal mechanism.

Changing a clock value does not necessarily mean aging itself has been reversed.

== Proteostasis ==

Proteostasis maintains protein folding, quality control, and turnover.

Age-associated decline can involve:

* damaged proteins;
* aggregates;
* altered chaperones;
* impaired degradation.

The proteostasis network includes the ubiquitin–proteasome system and autophagy.

Different tissues show different vulnerabilities.

== Autophagy ==

Autophagy delivers cellular material to lysosomes for degradation and recycling.

Macroautophagy contributes to:

* organelle turnover;
* nutrient recycling;
* protein quality control.

Age-associated changes in autophagy are implicated in multiple tissues.

The 2023 hallmarks framework separated disabled macroautophagy as its own hallmark.

== Nutrient sensing ==

Growth and metabolic pathways respond to nutrient state.

Important signaling systems include:

* insulin/IGF signaling;
* mTOR;
* AMPK;
* sirtuin-related pathways.

Manipulations of these pathways can extend lifespan in several model organisms.

Effects in humans are more complex, and lifespan extension in laboratory models should not be treated as proven human anti-aging therapy.

== Mitochondrial dysfunction ==

Mitochondria generate ATP and participate in:

* redox signaling;
* metabolism;
* apoptosis;
* innate immunity.

Aging can alter mitochondrial dynamics, quality control, and function.

Mitochondrial stress can also trigger adaptive responses.

“More mitochondrial activity” is therefore not a universal anti-aging rule.

== Cellular senescence ==

Cellular senescence is a persistent state of proliferative arrest initiated by stresses such as:

* telomere dysfunction;
* DNA damage;
* oncogene activation;
* oxidative stress;
* mitochondrial dysfunction.

Senescent cells remain metabolically active and can change tissue environments through secreted factors.

The state is heterogeneous.

No single marker identifies every senescent cell.

== Hayflick limit ==

Leonard Hayflick and Paul Moorhead showed that normal human diploid fibroblasts have a finite replicative lifespan in culture.

Repeated division leads to replicative senescence.

This challenged the assumption that normal cultured cells proliferate indefinitely.

The Hayflick limit is a cell-culture phenomenon connected partly to telomere shortening.

It is not a direct fixed lifespan limit for a human organism.

== Senescence-associated secretory phenotype ==

Many senescent cells develop a '''senescence-associated secretory phenotype''' (SASP).

SASP factors can include:

* cytokines;
* chemokines;
* growth factors;
* matrix-remodeling enzymes.

SASP composition depends on cell type, stress, and time.

It can promote inflammation or tissue remodeling and can influence neighboring cells.

There is no single universal SASP profile.

== Beneficial roles ==

Cellular senescence is not purely harmful.

Transient senescence can contribute to:

* embryonic development;
* wound healing;
* tumor suppression;
* tissue remodeling.

Cell-cycle arrest can prevent damaged cells from proliferating.

Problems can arise when senescent cells accumulate or persist chronically.

== Tumor suppression and cancer ==

Senescence can suppress tumors by stopping proliferation of damaged or oncogene-activated cells.

However, long-lasting SASP signaling can also alter the tissue microenvironment in ways that may support later tumor progression.

The relationship with cancer is therefore context-dependent.

== Immune clearance ==

Senescent cells can be recognized and removed by immune cells.

With age, clearance can become less effective.

This can contribute to accumulation.

The exact mechanisms vary across tissues and senescence states.

== Biomarkers ==

Common senescence-associated markers include:

* p16INK4a;
* p21;
* senescence-associated beta-galactosidase;
* persistent DNA-damage foci;
* altered lamin B1;
* SASP factors.

No single marker is definitive.

A rigorous study generally uses multiple markers plus evidence of durable proliferative arrest and appropriate context.

== Chronological versus biological age ==

Chronological age is time since birth.

'''Biological age''' is a model-dependent estimate of physiological state.

Proposed measures include:

* epigenetic clocks;
* proteomic profiles;
* clinical biomarkers;
* functional measures.

Different biological-age measures can disagree.

No one measure is universally accepted as the true age of an organism.

== Model organisms ==

Aging research uses:

* yeast;
* worms;
* flies;
* mice;
* other species.

These models allow controlled genetic and environmental experiments.

A lifespan intervention in a worm or mouse does not prove the same effect in humans.

Species differ in physiology and causes of death.

== Lifespan and healthspan ==

'''Lifespan''' is duration of life.

'''Healthspan''' is the period of life spent in relatively good health or function.

An intervention could extend lifespan without proportionally improving healthspan.

Aging research increasingly distinguishes these outcomes.

== Senolytics ==

'''Senolytics''' are interventions intended to preferentially eliminate senescent cells.

Animal studies have produced promising results for some age-related phenotypes.

Human research remains early and condition-specific.

No senolytic is established as a general anti-aging treatment for healthy people.

Claims of rejuvenation should be evaluated against controlled clinical evidence.

== Senomorphics ==

'''Senomorphics''' aim to modify harmful senescent-cell phenotypes, such as parts of the SASP, without necessarily killing the cells.

This could preserve beneficial senescence functions while reducing chronic inflammatory effects.

The concept remains an active research area.

== Lifestyle and aging ==

Factors such as:

* smoking;
* physical activity;
* diet;
* sleep;
* social environment;

are associated with health outcomes across aging.

These relationships do not collapse into one cellular-senescence mechanism.

Aging is a multilevel process involving behavior, disease, environment, and biology.

== Aging and disease ==

Age is a major risk factor for many diseases.

Aging itself is not identical to any one disease.

Mechanisms of aging can contribute to several diseases simultaneously.

Conversely, disease can accelerate functional decline.

This overlap motivates geroscience research.

== Senescence versus quiescence ==

'''Quiescence''' is a reversible nondividing state.

Cells can return to proliferation when conditions change.

Cellular senescence is generally much more durable.

The distinction matters experimentally because both can show low proliferation.

== Senescence versus apoptosis ==

'''Apoptosis''' is programmed cell death.

Senescent cells remain alive.

They can persist for long periods while altering the tissue environment.

The two programs can be triggered by related stresses but have different outcomes.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Senescence is an established biological phenomenon.

Stronger claims require care:

* one biomarker does not prove cellular senescence;
* one aging clock does not define biological age;
* lifespan extension in model organisms does not establish human benefit;
* removing senescent cells is not yet a validated universal rejuvenation therapy.

